  <section class="faq-section" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-heading"><div class="container"><div class="faq-layout"><div><div class="section-label">Common Questions</div><h2 class="section-title" id="faq-heading">Long Beach Mortgage FAQs</h2><p class="section-sub" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">Answers to questions I hear most from buyers in Long Beach.</p><div class="faq-list"><div class="faq-item"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">Which Long Beach zip code is best for first-time buyers?<span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button><div class="faq-answer"><p>90802 (Downtown, $509K Zillow median) and 90813 (north/central LB, $598K) are the most accessible for FHA buyers. 90804 ($690K) and 90810 ($714K) offer more SFR options at FHA-accessible price points. Which is best depends on your priorities — walkable downtown lifestyle (90802), more SFR character (90804), or commuter access (90810). A 20-minute call can help you figure out which one fits your life.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">Do I need a jumbo loan to buy in Belmont Shore or Naples?<span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button><div class="faq-answer"><p>In most cases, yes. The 90803 Zillow median is $1.32M — well above the $1.2M LA County conforming limit. Naples Island custom homes regularly push above $2M. Most meaningful Belmont Shore and Naples purchases require jumbo financing with 700+ credit, 20% down, and 12 months of reserves. That said, some smaller 90803 condos and townhomes still fall within conventional range.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">Is Long Beach a good investment market?<span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button><div class="faq-answer"><p>Long Beach has one of the highest renter percentages of any major California city — over 40% of households are renters. Strong demand comes from CSULB students, port and logistics workers, healthcare professionals, and commuters 